# Pages of www.nps.gov (Gettysburg National Military Park), fetched 2026-11-13 (tmp/library/web/nps/pages/<ID>/<ID>.txt).
# A page of an official body supports only what it literally says. The rehabilitation and natural-features pages describe the park as it is
# managed today and the park's own methods; they are NOT descriptions of the 1863 landscape unless a sentence says so.
# A repository key of the form R12.nnn names the terrain-source catalogue item the page answers (see GOVERNANCE/horde/sources-a, sources-b).
@ NPSGOV-BATTLEFIELD-REHAB
1 | general | Mapping resources named by the park for the 1863 landscape | R12.013 | ~maps prepared in 1863, 1868 and 1872 (as named on the page) | stated | The page says the park's most important mapping resources included Department of War and Gettysburg Battlefield Memorial Association maps prepared in 1863, 1868 and 1872, without naming the sheets | Our most important mapping resources included Department of War and Gettysburg Battlefield Memorial Association maps prepared in 1863, 1868, and 1872
1 | general | Method: base maps from archives, photographs, sketches, maps and aerial photographs | R12.013 | ~park research method (modern) | stated | The park's historians built a landscape history and historical base maps from park archival materials, library records, historic photographs and sketches, maps and, for the 20th century, aerial photographs | These maps were based upon extensive research, including park archival materials >>> aerial photographs.
1 | fences | Nine miles of fence rebuilt: Pickett's Charge field as 12 small fields | R12.016 | ~2000-2010 rebuilt fences (modern); describes a historic configuration | stated | The page says nine miles of fences have been rebuilt, showing the field of Pickett's Charge in its historic configuration of 12 small fields | Now, nine miles of fences have been rebuilt, showing the field of Pickett's Charge in its historic configuration of 12 small fields
1 | woodlots | Non-historic trees grown up over 65-70 years to be removed | R12.016 | ~modern (growth since about 1940) | stated | The page says non-historic trees that have grown up over the past 65-70 years are to be removed to restore the 1863 characteristics of observation, fields of fire, cover and concealment | we need to remove non-historic trees that have grown up over the past 65 - 70 years >>> cover and concealment
1 | fields | Fields not farmed for 65 years have become forests | R12.016 | ~modern (growth since about 1940) | stated | The page says fields that have not been farmed over the past 65 years have become forests | fields that have not been farmed over the past 65 years have become forests
1 | fences | Small-scale features: fences, orchards, open woodlots, buildings | R12.016 | 1863-07 (significance of small-scale features) | stated | The page says small-scale features such as fences, orchards, open woodlots and buildings affected the tactical movements of small units and the cover and obstacles soldiers met | Small-scale features, such as fences, orchards, open woodlots and buildings, affected the tactical movements of small units
1 | fields | Project start and plan | R12.016 | ~2000 (project start) | stated | The page says the rehabilitation project started in 2000 after the General Management Plan and calls for re-establishing grasslands, restoring wetlands and replanting orchards | We started the project in 2000, after completing a General Management plan/environmental impact statement.
@ NPSGOV-NATURAL-FEATURES
1 | general | Park area | R12.042 | modern (page as served 2026) | stated | The page gives the park as 5,989 acres | The park’s 5,989 acres provide opportunities
1 | fields | Crop, pasture and meadow acreage today | R12.042 | modern (page as served 2026) | stated | Over 2,300 acres of the park are planted in crops, pasture or meadows today | Over 2,300 acres of the park’s landscape are planted in crops, pasture, or meadows
1 | woodlots | Woodlot and forest acreage today | R12.042 | modern (page as served 2026) | stated | Over 1,600 acres are woodlots and forested habitat, from mature oak/hickory to early scrub-shrub | Over 1,600 acres of woodlots and forested habitat comprise several successional communities, from mature oak/hickory to early scrub-shrub.
1 | general | Wetlands today | R12.042 | modern (page as served 2026) | stated | The page gives roughly 148 acres of palustrine wetland and over 26 miles of riparian habitat | roughly totaling 148 acres of palustrine wetland and over 26 miles of associated riparian habitat
1 | woodlots | Forest regeneration study since 1985 | R12.042 | modern (study since 1985) | stated | The page says studies of forest regeneration in the park's woodlots have continued since 1985 | Ongoing studies to understand forest regeneration in woodlots at Gettysburg has been a continuing study since 1985.
1 | woodlots | 1999 GMP/EIS: replanting missing historic woodlots, orchards and thickets | R12.042 | 1999 plan (modern) | stated | The 1999 GMP/EIS set goals for rehabilitating 1863 features, including replanting missing historic woodlots, orchards and thickets and removing non-historic vegetation | In 1999, the Gettysburg National Military Park GMP/EIS was approved >>> removal of non-historic vegetation.
@ NPSGOV-PLANTS
1 | woodlots | Dominant overstory species of the woodlots today | R12.043 | modern (page as served 2026; not an 1863 species list) | stated | The page names white oak, white ash and northern red oak as the dominant overstory species documented in the park's woodlots and woodlands today | the dominant overstory species documented in woodlots and woodlands are white oak, white ash, and northern red oak
1 | general | Vascular plants today | R12.043 | modern (page as served 2026) | stated | The page counts 553 vascular plant species in the park, 410 native | 553 species of vascular plants, 410 of which are native
